{
  "abstract": "Finite quadratic interpolation determinants impose only a bounded number of pure Hasse derivative conditions. We describe their universal local lattice in three regimes while retaining the original weighted support $r+Ks<R$. For the unramified quadratic integers at $p$, put $q=p^2$. In the balanced slope range $q/(p-1)\\le K\\le q(p-1)$, a paired digit construction gives an exact evaluation basis with leading denominators $s+v_p(s!)+m+v_p(m!)$, where $m=\\lfloor r/q\\rfloor$. An explicit whole-lattice Mahler comparison then determines the cubic leading index for every derivative budget $J=o(R)$.\n\nFor $p<K\\le p^3$, multiplication by a divided Frobenius lift gives a compatible isomorphism of relative saturation layers under $R\\mapsto R+Kqa$, $J\\mapsto J+p^3a$, through depth $v_p(a)+1$. At a complete Taylor block $J=p^3a$, those initial layer dimensions coincide, and their first decrease is an explicit boundary count. Keeping the next residue digit resolves the extra dyadic carry. For bounded admissible slack $B$, the full quotient at $R=Kqa+B$ is a direct sum of $|\\Lambda_B|$ copies of $\\mathcal O/p^{v_p(a)+1}\\mathcal O$. Thus fixed-depth periodicity coexists with unbounded full depth. Exact coefficient, Mahler and sparse-rank controls accompany the proofs. The all-order ghost basis, digit principle and derivative-denominator methods are imported and credited. The complete index at a fixed interior ratio remains open; no new cubic gain there, discriminant endpoint, census or classification is claimed.",
